No core router crash is occurring. End-to-end reachability is healthy (pc0 -> pc12: 0% loss, ~1 ms RTT both directions). Both “middle” routers are up and routing normally. On r6, all uplinks are up and OSPF adjacencies are Full: neighbors 46.1.1.1 on eth0 (to r4), 56.1.1.1 on eth1 (to r5), and 67.1.1.2 on eth2 (to r7) are Full with normal dead timers. The FRR route table is populated with broad OSPF coverage and ECMP where expected (e.g., 45.0.0.0/8 via both 46.1.1.1 and 56.1.1.1). On r7, interfaces are up and OSPF to r6 (neighbor 67.1.1.1 on eth0) is Full/Backup, with extensive learned routes installed. There is no evidence of blackholing: if a “middle” node were crashed or wedged, we would see down OSPF neighbors and loss or routing holes, which are absent here.

Localization: r6 (eth0=S, eth1=V, eth2=Y) and r7 (eth0=Y) are stable; OSPF processes are up and exchanging LSAs. Mechanism: stable adjacencies and full routing tables allow normal forwarding across the core; traffic is not “eaten.”

Root cause: No device failure—this appears to be a false positive monitoring alert (possibly from a transient flap or mis-threshold).

Corrective fix: Do not reboot. Clear the alert and adjust monitoring to trigger on definitive signals (interface down/OSPF neighbor down) and correlate with router logs; continue to monitor but no remediation is required on r6 or r7. 
